    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. what about the part where it covers \"unforeseeable circumstances\"? if a tenant claims that the chaos/unpredictability of a specific situation was just too much to manage, how does the court usually view that threshold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Courts generally interpret \"unforeseeable circumstances\" narrowly, requiring the event to be truly outside the tenant's reasonable control and not a normal business risk. A mere increase in operational difficulty or financial strain is usually insufficient to qualify; the event must prevent performance altogether, not just make it difficult or costly. The threshold is high, demanding objective evidence that the circumstances were genuinely unpredictable and could not have been reasonably guarded against.",
      "source": "target",
